# C#

Formatting is CSharpier's (`.cursor/rules/engineering.mdc`). This rule covers what a formatter cannot.

## Style

- Modern C# is allowed: `var` when the type is apparent on the right, collection expressions, target-typed `new`, primary constructors, file-scoped namespaces, `required` members, `ref struct`, `scoped`.
- Private fields are `_camelCase`, static ones included. Constants are PascalCase. No `this.` unless needed to disambiguate. Interfaces start with `I`.
- Namespaces follow assembly names, not folders. No type has the name of a namespace (CA1724 is an error).
- One type per file by default. A file name matches its main type.
- No `using` alias to shorten a BCL type name. Qualify the name where it collides.
- Library `await` uses `ConfigureAwait(false)`. `CancellationToken` is the last parameter.
- A new overload goes above the XML docs and attributes of the member it joins, not between them and that member.

## Types

Make an invalid state unrepresentable; do not document it away or catch it at run time.

- One choice, one slot: an enum, a sealed record hierarchy, or a generic parameter, not a `bool` plus a nullable that the `bool` makes meaningless.
- A value that has been verified has its own type (a verified license is not the same type as a parsed, unverified token), so unverified data cannot reach a decision.
- Identifiers are distinct types (`LicenseId`, `LeaseId`, `KeyId`), so one cannot be passed where another is expected. The `default` of an ID type never refers to a live value.
- A method that cannot fail returns its value, not a `bool`/`out` pair or a nullable.
- A public method does not throw because the caller used the wrong phase of the same type; split the type instead.
- The public surface is what the design lists. Everything else is `internal`.

## Errors

- An expected outcome that a caller decides on (a license that is expired, a seat that is not free, a signature that does not verify) is a return value with a reason the design lists, not an exception.
- Argument checks throw `ArgumentException` and its subtypes, with one-sentence English messages that name the condition.
- Public exception types are few and documented in the XML docs of the members that throw them.
- A `catch` that runs cleanup which can throw keeps the exception it caught: when the cleanup throws too, throw an `AggregateException` with the caught exception first, then the cleanup's.
- A comment that states how a runtime or OS API behaves names what was checked: the call, the version, and the result.
- A comment that states an order ("before everything that writes to the folder") is checked against the lines above it in the same method. In V1d2 the comment on the server's lock said that the lock comes before everything that writes to the data folder, while `TryLoadOrCreate` above it creates the folder and the installation key (PR #355, the owner's review, S1).
- A call that can fail by its result is checked (`TryParse`, `TryImport...`, `TryGetValue`).
- Code that reads an answer of our own programs with `JsonDocument` reads a nullable member that the writer's JSON context leaves out when `null` (`DefaultIgnoreCondition = WhenWritingNull`) with `TryGetProperty`, and a test covers the answer without it. `elm server status` threw for a server license without expiry because `elm-server` leaves out `licenseExpires` and the CLI read it with `GetProperty` (V1c7, PR #207).
- A refactor that moves checks into a shared function compares each message the callers returned before and after. A changed message is listed under Risk in the PR. In M4d1 three refusals of `elm-server` changed wording when its checks moved to `LeaseRules`, and one place kept the old wording (PR #83).

## Cryptography objects

- Measure what creating a cryptography object costs against what one operation costs, under NativeAOT, before putting it on a measured path. In M1, creating an `ECDsa` and importing a P-256 public key took about 275 µs on Windows, against about 110 µs for one verification, and verification took 888 µs per license file until each key was created once (PR #8).
- State that another thread or a test reads (a new token, a state change) is made visible last, after everything that follows from it is set up (the next timer, a record). After M3c the client showed a renewed token before it scheduled the next renewal, and a test that advanced its fake clock as soon as it saw the token failed on the Windows runner of `main` (PR #32).
- A timer whose due time can be zero is created with an infinite due time, stored in its field, and then given its due time with `Change`. A timer due at once can run its callback inside `TimeProvider.CreateTimer`, before the field holds it: in M4b2 `FakeTimeProvider` did so, and the callback saw no timer to replace (PR #67).
- A key that verifies many tokens is created once and kept by its owner. The documentation does not promise that one `ECDsa` may be used on several threads at once, so a shared key takes a lock. A key that one call creates is disposed by that call; a shared key is not disposed while another call may hold it.
- A cache lives in an owner that outlives the calls that use it, never in a local that one call creates. A test counts the objects created and checks that a second call creates none. In M3a, `VerifyHosted` made a new `CertificateSet` on each call, so its cache never hit and the keys it created were never disposed; the owner's review found it (PR #16).

## Native memory

- The length of a C string comes from `MemoryMarshal.CreateReadOnlySpanFromNullTerminated`, not from a search in a span of a made-up length. A span of `int.MaxValue` bytes lets the vectorized search read past the NUL into a page that may not be mapped; the owner's review of M6b found it in `elm_string_free` (PR #142).

## XML docs

- Public members under `src/` have `summary`, and `param` / `returns` / `exception` when a caller might be unsure. Sentences end with a period. Properties start with "Gets" / "Gets or sets"; constructors with "Initializes a new instance of the <see cref="T"/> class".
- XML docs state the caller's contract only. How it works lives in `docs/design.ja.md`; do not restate it. In `ElasticLM.Licensing` and `ElasticLM.Client` the XML docs, those of non-public members included, hold no identifier that only this repository explains (a design section or decision number, a threat or attacker ID, a roadmap row, an Issue or PR number, the name of a test): the reference goes into one `//` comment above the doc block, with an empty line between them (SA1514). Their XML documentation files ship in the packages, and a user cannot look any of these up. A string literal under `src/` holds none either; its reference goes into a `//` comment above the statement. The XML docs of the other projects may name the section. A public standard stays, written as `RFC 7515 §2`. The tests `InternalReferencesTests` and `ExportsTests.Header_EveryLine_HoldsNoInternalIdentifier` fail on such an identifier, and the design lists what they do not find (design §20.7, the V1c6 Grill; Issue #198).
- Use `<inheritdoc />` when an implementation has the interface's contract.
- Wording and terminology: `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`.
